  m_FontData:
    m_Font: {fileID: 12800000, guid: 5a8ef543591268943a212da7372c3294, type: 3}
    m_FontSize: 65
    m_FontStyle: 0
    m_BestFit: 0
    m_MinSize: 0
    m_MaxSize: 186
    m_Alignment: 4
    m_AlignByGeometry: 0
    m_RichText: 1
    m_HorizontalOverflow: 0
    m_VerticalOverflow: 0
    m_LineSpacing: 1
  m_Text: The Largemouth Bass (Micropterus salmoides) is one of the most important
    freshwater gamefish in the United States. A member of the sunfish family and
    a species of black bass native to North america. Able to live for up to 16 years
    and distinguished by their longer upper jaw, which, when closed, extends beyond
    the eye. They are the largest of the black basses, with the largest recorded
    overall length reaching 29.5 inches. Residing in the water of the Everglades
    their diet consists mainly of smaller fish, snails, crawfish, frogs, snakes,
    salamanders, bats, and even small water birds, mammals, and baby alligators.
    As one of the top predators in the waters they play an important job at maintaining
    the food webs balance. Without bass, the minnow population would soar out of
    control, and there would be overconsumption of zooplankton.
